# Stagepass PHP boilerplate

Plain PHP 8.2 or newer. No framework. No Composer. No mail.

`api/action.php` is the only public entry. Every request names an `action`. That name is looked up in `api/actions.php` and then handed to a file in `api/actions/`. User input is never used as a file path or a function name.

A longer guide is at [https://stagepass.one/docs/php/](https://stagepass.one/docs/php/). Copy `api/.env.example` to `.env` in the project root, or use `.php/.env`. `stagepass build` never copies `.env` into `dist`.

## Where to edit

Edit these:

```
api/actions.php         Allowlist: register a new action here
api/actions/*.php       Your action logic (see contact.php)
.env                    Origins, limits, APP_ENV (copy from api/.env.example)
.php/config.php         Optional PHP overrides of the .env defaults
```

Leave these alone unless you are changing the core:

```
api/action.php          Front controller
api/lib/*               Responses, request parsing, security, validation, config
```

## Layout

```
api/action.php          Public front controller (core)
api/actions.php         Action allowlist (you edit this)
api/actions/ping.php    GET health check
api/actions/contact.php POST form handler (validates only, does not send mail)
api/lib/                Shared helpers (core)
api/.env.example        Copy to the project root as .env
.php/config.php         Optional overrides, inside the document root (the folder that contains api/)
.php/rate-limit/        Runtime files, not copied to dist. Deny hidden paths on the host
.htaccess               Apache deny for hidden paths (shipped)
.env                    Local environment (do not commit)
```

## Configure

1. Copy `api/.env.example` to `.env` in the project root (the folder that contains `api/`).
2. Set `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` to the exact site origins that may call this API, including the scheme and without a trailing slash. Typical values are `https://your-project.sp`, `https://your-site.webflow.io`, and `https://www.example.com`.
3. Keep `.env` out of git. The document root is the folder that contains `api/` (the project root locally, `dist/` after `stagepass build`). Caddy returns 404 for any path segment that starts with `.`. Since 2.4.1 this starter ships a web-root `.htaccess` (Apache needs `AllowOverride All` and `mod_rewrite`). `stagepass upgrade` writes that file when `stagepass.json` has `"php": true` and the project root has none. An existing `.htaccess` is left unchanged. If it has no `RewriteRule "(^|/)\." - [F]` line, add that rule yourself. Published CLI 2.4.0 does not write the file. `stagepass build` copies the project `.htaccess` into `dist/` and does not replace an existing project `.htaccess`. It does not copy `.env`, `.php/.env`, or `.php/rate-limit/`. nginx does not read `.htaccess`. Put this in the server block before `location ~ \.php$`:

   ```nginx
   location ~* /\.(?!well-known\/) {
       deny all;
   }
   ```

   Prefer real environment variables on the host so a `.env` file is not required in the web root.
4. `stagepass build` copies `api/` to `dist/api/`, `.php/config.php` to `dist/.php/config.php`, and the project `.htaccess` to `dist/.htaccess`. Upload `dist/`. The public URL stays `/api/action.php`.

`APP_ENV=local` turns on a hidden `boom` action used only to test error handling. Leave `APP_ENV=production` on a live host.

## Add a new action

1. Create `api/actions/newsletter.php`. The file must return a callable. Read fields with `input_string()`. Throw `ValidationException` when input is invalid (that is a 422 fail). Any other uncaught exception becomes a generic 500 error.

```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

if (!defined('STAGEPASS_PHP_ENTRY')) {
    http_response_code(404);
    exit;
}

return static function (array $input, array $config): array {
    $errors = [];
    validate_required($input, 'email', $errors);
    validate_email($input, 'email', $errors);
    if ($errors !== []) {
        throw new ValidationException($errors);
    }
    // Your logic here.
    return ['subscribed' => true];
};
```

2. Register the name in `api/actions.php`:

```php
'newsletter' => [
    'file' => 'newsletter.php',
    'methods' => ['POST'],
    'honeypot' => true,
    'rate_limit' => true,
    'origin' => 'required',
],
```

3. Call it from JavaScript with `action: 'newsletter'`. Do not add a second public PHP file unless you have a strong reason.

## Call it from JavaScript

`sp.resolveLocalUrl` returns a local HTTPS URL only during an active `.sp` session, otherwise `''`. On a Webflow page `window.location.origin` is the Webflow host, and Webflow does not run PHP. There is no helper for the public PHP base URL (`origin` and `production` in `stagepass.json` are Webflow hosts). Use the absolute URL of the PHP host, for example `https://php.example.com/api/action.php`.

```js
const path = '/api/action.php';
const phpHost = 'https://php.example.com';
const local = sp.vars.isLocal && sp.resolveLocalUrl(path);
const url = local || phpHost + path;

const response = await fetch(url, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    action: 'contact',
    name: 'Ada Lovelace',
    email: 'ada@example.com',
    message: 'Hello',
    website: '', // honeypot; leave empty, hide this field in CSS
  }),
});

const result = await response.json();
// result.status is "success", "fail", or "error"
if (result.status === 'success') {
  console.log('Saved', result.data);
} else {
  console.warn(result.message, result.data);
}
```

A simple GET check:

```js
const url =
  (sp.vars.isLocal && sp.resolveLocalUrl('/api/action.php')) ||
  'https://php.example.com/api/action.php';

const result = await fetch(url + '?action=ping', {
  headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
}).then((r) => r.json());
```

`contact.php` validates a JSON or form POST and does not send mail. A native Webflow form needs its own submit listener. There is no shipped helper for that.

```js
form.addEventListener('submit', async (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const url =
    (sp.vars.isLocal && sp.resolveLocalUrl('/api/action.php')) ||
    'https://php.example.com/api/action.php';
  await fetch(url, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Accept: 'application/json', 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      action: 'contact',
      name: form.elements.name.value,
      email: form.elements.email.value,
      message: form.elements.message.value,
      website: '',
    }),
  });
});
```

## Response shape

Every response is JSON with `status`, `data`, and `message`, plus a matching HTTP status code.

```json
{ "status": "success", "data": { "pong": true }, "message": null }
```

```json
{
  "status": "fail",
  "data": { "email": "A valid email is required." },
  "message": "Validation failed."
}
```

```json
{
  "status": "error",
  "data": null,
  "message": "An unexpected error occurred. Try again later."
}
```

`fail` is a rejected request (validation 422, unknown action 404, wrong method 405, disallowed origin 403, too many requests 429). `error` is an unexpected server failure (500) and never includes internal details.

## Security defaults

POST actions require an `Origin` header that is on `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`. GET `ping` may omit `Origin`, but a present `Origin` still has to match the list. CORS headers are only sent for allowed origins. A filled honeypot field (`website` by default) returns a generic success and does not run the handler. Rate limiting is per IP and action, stored as files under `.php/rate-limit/`. A limit hit is HTTP 429, JSend `fail`, and `Retry-After` set to the window. If the directory cannot be created, a file cannot be opened, `flock` fails, or the limiter throws, the failure is logged, including a failed `flock`. The response is HTTP 503, JSend `error`, a short message, `Retry-After: 60`, and no internals, unless `APP_ENV` is `local`, `development`, or `dev` (trimmed, any case). Empty, unset, and every other value stay closed. Those three names allow the request and write the same log line. `client_ip()` uses `REMOTE_ADDR` unless that address is in `TRUSTED_PROXIES` (comma-separated IPv4 or IPv6 addresses and CIDRs). Only then is `TRUSTED_PROXY_HEADER` read: `x-forwarded-for` (default) or `forwarded` (RFC 7239). Hops are walked from right to left; trusted proxy addresses are skipped; the first other address is the client. Hops to the left of the client are ignored. An invalid hop in the trusted-proxy suffix falls back to `REMOTE_ADDR` and is logged once without the raw header. Projects created before this behaviour keep their PHP files. `stagepass upgrade` does not edit `api/`. Copy `api/lib/security.php`, `api/lib/request.php`, `api/lib/config.php`, `.php/config.php`, and `api/action.php` from a current scaffold, or re-scaffold.

## Local PHP server

From the project root (the folder that contains `api/`):

```bash
php -S 127.0.0.1:8080 -t .
```

Then: `curl 'http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/action.php?action=ping'`

`php -S 127.0.0.1:8080 -t api` serves `/action.php` and does not match this layout. PHP's built-in server does not return 404 for hidden paths the way Caddy does (`/.env`, `/.php/`). Do not point `php -S -t .` at a folder that already contains a real `.env`. Prefer `stagepass dev` or the linked `.sp` domain.

On a linked Stagepass domain the same file is reached at `https://your-project.sp/api/action.php`.
